     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-11 · editor 7/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Cyclone Isaias kills four as it moves north through U.S.</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Post-tropical cyclone Isaias has resulted in at least four deaths across the southeastern United States after making landfall near Destin, Florida, as a Category 2 hurricane. The storm brought flooding, tornado risks, and power outages to 440,000 customers across Florida, Alabama, and Georgia.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/cyclone-isaias-kills-four-as-it-moves-north-through-us.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The National Hurricane Center warned of potential flash flooding and landslides as the storm tracks inland toward the Southern Appalachians. Officials have reported storm-related fatalities caused by falling trees and improper generator use. The U.S. Department of Energy authorized the release of 4 million barrels of reserve crude to mitigate impacts on the Gulf Coast oil refining hub.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief omits that the storm moved inland and caused fatalities from falling trees, and it includes extra details about the US DOE that were not in the source summary (though present in the full text).</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-dead-cyclone-isaias-drenches-southern.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
