     <article class="story single" data-beat="entertainment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENTERTAINMENT</span> · TOI Entertainment · 2026-10-11 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Ethan Hawke reveals decade of career disillusionment following Dead Poets Society</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#entertainment</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Actor Ethan Hawke recently shared that he struggled for a decade after his breakout role in the 1989 film Dead Poets Society. During a book talk for Kyle MacLachlan, Hawke explained that he initially believed the positive, collaborative atmosphere on the set of the Peter Weir film was the industry standard. </p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/ethan-hawke-reveals-decade-of-career-disillusionment-followi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>He soon faced a reality check, discovering that many subsequent projects were characterized by cold, manipulative, and unkind environments. Hawke noted that while he continued to find work, he did not experience another set environment that matched the quality and respect he encountered during his early success for ten years.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately captures the essence of Hawke's interview, noting the specific duration of his disillusionment and the contrast between his first set experience and subsequent industry realities.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/entertainment/english/hollywood/news/in-1989-ethan-hawke-mesmerised-all-with-dead-poets-society-but-for-10-years-after-that-he-did-not-have-good-time-on-set-he-says-people-are-really-cold-and-crass/articleshow/134867837.cms" rel="noopener">TOI Entertainment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
