     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · News18 India · 2026-10-11 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Illicit Starlink Terminals Prompt India's Security Review Amid Global Black Market Concerns</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Starlink</span> <span>#National Security</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#SpaceX</span></div>
       <p>India is intensifying regulatory scrutiny of SpaceX’s Starlink before granting commercial approval, driven by national security concerns arising from a global black market for its terminals. The government's cautious stance follows revelations of security vulnerabilities worldwide, with illicit Starlink devices bypassing permissions and raising risks.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/illicit-starlink-terminals-prompt-indias-security-review-ami.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Recent investigations have highlighted these vulnerabilities. A Haaretz newspaper report on May 12, 2026, detailed how secure Starlink traffic could be mapped using Mobile Advertising IDs (MAID). This investigation uncovered how two Israeli intelligence firms, TargetTeam and Rayzone, bypassed terminal encryption through ground-based data fusion, unmasking hundreds of thousands of hidden terminals globally, including those operating in sensitive, black-market zones in India.</p><p>India has already experienced incidents involving illicit Starlink use. During the Manipur Insurgency Seizure on December 13, 2024, active Starlink terminals were recovered from the banned People’s Liberation Army (PLA) insurgent group in Imphal East. SpaceX CEO Elon Musk explicitly denied these claims on December 17, 2024, stating on X that Starlink satellite beams were entirely turned off over India. Another incident in November 2024 saw the interception of a Myanmar fishing trawler carrying 6,000 kg of methamphetamine, valued at ₹36,000 crore, along with a Starlink Mini terminal in the Andaman &amp; Nicobar region. A Press Trust of India report on November 28, 2024, confirmed this as India's first documented case of smugglers using Starlink hotspots for open-sea navigation.</p><p>The black market problem extends beyond India. In October 2024, a raid uncovered hundreds of active rogue Starlink kits within the KK Park cyber-scam network in Myawaddy, Myanmar. This prompted enforcement actions, leading SpaceX to officially deactivate over 2,500 illicit terminals near suspected Southeast Asian scam hubs by October 2025, as documented by Moneylife India and The Register.</p><p>Investigations in 2024 by Bloomberg and The Wall Street Journal revealed a systemic global issue, with thousands of unauthorized Starlink terminals operating in unlicensed, banned, or sanctioned territories. Despite SpaceX's technical ability to geolocate and deactivate kits, illicit hardware remained accessible in conflict zones and restricted nations. The black market exploited standard consumer features: smugglers legally purchased hardware in authorized countries, activated devices under roaming plans registered to licensed zones, allowing connectivity even in restricted areas. Online traders openly marketed hardware in WhatsApp and Facebook groups, offering tutorials on physical camouflage.</p><p>SpaceX initiated crackdowns, terminating unauthorized roaming access in regions like South Africa and Sudan. However, this policy created a humanitarian dilemma by potentially cutting off vital communication lifelines for aid agencies, doctors, and civilians in conflict zones. Unauthorized hardware appeared in Sudan, where the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF) used smuggled kits from Gulf countries for tactical communication. In Russia and Ukraine, frontline Russian forces acquired terminals via global black markets and eBay, eroding Ukraine’s technical advantages, despite SpaceX maintaining it did not do business with the Russian government or military.</p><p>To counter the issue, the Pentagon and Ukrainian intelligence cooperated with SpaceX to build a verification “whitelist” to selectively shut down unauthorized signals on active battlefields. Concurrently, various African nations, including South Africa and Zimbabwe, implemented strict local crackdowns, confiscating smuggled kits and issuing warnings. In India, the Ministry of Home Affairs, Department of Telecommunications, and national security agencies are holding up Starlink’s commercial launch due to deep structural anxieties regarding sovereignty, national security, and regulatory compliance.</p><p>What to watch: India's government continues security reviews for Starlink's commercial approval.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is comprehensive, accurately reflecting all source details including specific dates, names of firms, and the context of the regulatory review.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/world/how-starlink-black-market-bypasses-permissions-and-risks-national-security-ws-klrt-10377054.html" rel="noopener">News18 India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
