     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · NDTV Sports · 2026-10-11 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India A Collapse Against Australia A in 3rd Unofficial ODI, Gaikwad Under Pressure</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Sports</span> <span>#Cricket</span> <span>#India A</span> <span>#Australia A</span></div>
       <p>India A's innings dramatically derailed in the third and final unofficial one-day match against Australia A in Puducherry on Sunday, after an excellent start. The team suffered a significant collapse, losing five wickets for just 35 runs, placing immense pressure on captain Ruturaj Gaikwad.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/india-a-collapse-against-australia-a-in-3rd-unofficial-odi-g.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>At the time of reporting, India A stood at 169/6 after 32 overs, with Suryansh Shedge and Vipraj Nigam at the crease. The innings began promisingly with openers Priyansh Arya (49) and Prabhsimran Singh (39) forging an explosive 83-run partnership, though both narrowly missed out on half-centuries. Australia A, however, clawed back into the game with pacemen Liam Scott and Ben Dwarshuis each claiming two crucial wickets.</p><p>Key dismissals further hampered India A's progress. Prabhsimran Singh was caught off Liam Scott for 39. Devdutt Padikkal fell cheaply to Scott, and Priyansh Arya was dismissed for 49, just one run shy of his fifty, after nicking a short delivery from Ben Dwarshuis to the wicket-keeper. Rajat Patidar, who had scored two consecutive fifties, managed only 3 runs before being caught and bowled by Dwarshuis. Nishant Sindhu also departed quickly after hitting a boundary, caught and bowled by Will Sutherland.</p><p>Captain Ruturaj Gaikwad, after making a cautious start, was dismissed for 26 runs off 49 balls, inside-edging onto the stumps. Despite the collapse, India A's batting depth still offers some hope, with Suryansh Shedge (an all-rounder), Vipraj Nigam, and Anukul Roy considered handy batters. The team's priority shifted to surviving the middle overs without further wicket losses, aiming for a total of at least 250.</p><p>India A is currently striving for a consolation victory, having already lost the series after suffering defeats in the first two matches. Australia A, led by Todd Murphy, is now in a strong position to achieve a series whitewash. The local crowd in Puducherry witnessed Gaikwad's efforts to stabilize the innings, including a six off Nikhil Chaudhary, and Vipraj Nigam's subsequent boundaries.</p><p>What to watch: Whether India A's remaining batters can build a competitive total to salvage a win and avoid a series whitewash.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a detailed and accurate summary of the cricket match status, capturing all key player dismissals and the current game situation described in the source.</p>
