     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-11 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Nobel-Winning Optogenetics Advances Parasite Research</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The 2026 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, awarded for discoveries regarding light-gated ion channels, has highlighted the expanding utility of optogenetics. This technique, which uses light-sensitive proteins to control biological processes, was pioneered by Nobel laureates Karl Deisseroth, Peter Hegemann, and Georg Nagel.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/nobel-winning-optogenetics-advances-parasite-research.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Nishith Gupta, a professor at BITS Pilani, Hyderabad, utilized his long-term collaboration with Hegemann to apply these light-based tools to the study of Toxoplasma gondii. By enabling the precise control of cells, optogenetics is transforming research into fundamental biological processes, neurological disorders, and infectious diseases.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief correctly links the Nobel-winning optogenetics research to the specific applications in parasite studies conducted by Nishith Gupta.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/telangana/berlin-to-bits-pilani-how-nobel-winning-optogenetics-connects-to-parasite-research-in-hyderabad/article71570522.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
