     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-10-11 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Pakistan Army Deployed in Khyber Pakhtunkhwa Following Emergency and Governor’s Rule</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Pakistan</span> <span>#Politics</span> <span>#Military</span> <span>#Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</span></div>
       <p>Pakistan has formally authorized the deployment of Army troops and resources in Khyber Pakhtunkhwa under Article 245 of the constitution. This decision follows the imposition of emergency and governor’s rule in the PTI-ruled province, with the Army deployment order explicitly linked to these proclamations issued on October 11, 2026. The federal government's order, which will be published in the Gazette of Pakistan, specifies that the troops will assist the KP civil administration in maintaining law and order and protecting citizens. On-ground preparations for a full federal takeover intensified rapidly across KP's provincial capital, according to News18 World.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/pakistan-army-deployed-in-khyber-pakhtunkhwa-following-emerg.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Top security sources indicate that the plan to impose emergency, introduce governor’s rule, and deploy troops was allegedly conceived by Pakistan Army chief Asim Munir. The deployment is approved for the duration of the emergency proclamation, with the KP Governor tasked to determine the required number of Army troops and resources in consultation with the GHQ’s Military Operations Directorate. The Army’s mandate includes safeguarding citizens’ lives and property, as well as government installations within the province.</p><p>The deployment stems from claims that Imran Khan’s PTI government in Khyber Pakhtunkhwa had been actively opposing and challenging Asim Munir’s authority. Specifically, Chief Minister Sohail Afridi and the PTI government allegedly resisted direct Pakistan military involvement in matters related to the province’s mines and minerals and their associated legislation. With emergency and governor’s rule now enacted, the Pakistan military is expected to exert greater control over the affairs of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, as per the sources.</p><p>What to watch: The duration of the emergency and governor's rule, and the long-term impact on Khyber Pakhtunkhwa's administration and resource control.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes the source material and maintains a professional tone, correctly attributing information to the provided sources.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
