     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · ScienceDaily · 2026-10-11 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Scientists Discover Hidden Eye Drainage Pathway</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers from the University of British Columbia and the University of Toronto have identified a previously unknown waste removal system in the eye called the posterior ocular lymphatic outflow (POLO) pathway. This drainage route allows fluid and metabolic waste to exit the back of the eye into the body's lymphatic system.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/scientists-discover-hidden-eye-drainage-pathway.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The discovery, demonstrated in mice, challenges existing models of ocular fluid regulation. By clarifying how the eye manages waste, this finding provides a new framework for understanding and potentially treating blinding conditions such as glaucoma and macular degeneration, which are often linked to fluid and inflammatory material accumulation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the discovery of the POLO pathway in mice and its potential implications for ocular disease research.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/10/261008005254.htm" rel="noopener">ScienceDaily</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
