     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-10-11 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Silicon Valley firms adopt Trump's 'super intelligence' terminology</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Prominent Silicon Valley leaders are adopting President Donald Trump’s preferred term, &quot;super intelligence,&quot; for artificial intelligence. Elon Musk, Marc Benioff, and Microsoft’s Satya Nadella have begun using the label, signaling a shift in how major tech companies refer to the technology.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/silicon-valley-firms-adopt-trumps-super-intelligence-termino.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang also aligned with the administration, including &quot;super intelligence research&quot; in a recent $1 billion funding commitment. This trend marks a significant effort by major technology firms to ingratiate themselves with the Trump administration during his second term, following a previously contentious relationship.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Well-summarized, though it omits the specific detail that the term change was mandated by an executive order.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1009406/silicon-valley-is-falling-in-line-with-trumps-attempt-to-rename-ai" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
