      <button id="tdp-theme" title="Toggle light/dark">Light</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <nav class="catrow"><span class="catgrp"><b>News</b><a href="/category/national">national</a><a href="/category/world">world</a><a href="/category/defence">defence</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Money</b><a href="/category/business">business</a><a href="/category/finance">finance</a><a href="/category/education">education</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Sports</b><a href="/category/sports">sports</a><a href="/category/cricket">cricket</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Culture</b><a href="/category/entertainment">entertainment</a><a href="/category/positive">positive</a><a href="/category/funny">funny</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Life</b><a href="/category/science">science</a><a href="/category/environment">environment</a><a href="/category/space">space</a><a href="/category/health">health</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Tech</b><a href="/category/tech">tech</a><a href="/category/ai">ai</a></span></nav></div><div class="gtr"><span>Read in your language:</span><div id="google_translate_element"></div></div><div class="wrap"><main id="content"><p class="crumb"><a href="/">← Front page</a> · <a href="/archive">Archive</a> · <a href="/category/national">national</a></p>
     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-11 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Supreme Court Addresses Mass Detentions and Transit Shutdown Amid Anti-CEC Protests</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#National</span> <span>#Protest</span> <span>#Supreme Court</span> <span>#Delhi</span></div>
       <p>New Delhi experienced significant anti-Chief Election Commissioner (CEC) protests on October 11, 2026, orchestrated by the Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) to demand CEC Gyanesh Kumar's resignation over alleged electoral irregularities. The CJP had called for another round of 'Chalo Jantar Mantar' protest for Sunday, following the detention of thousands of individuals. Security personnel and police vehicles were visibly stationed across the capital to manage law and order.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/supreme-court-addresses-mass-detentions-and-transit-shutdown.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>On Sunday morning, CJP leaders Abhijeet Dipke, Saurav Das, and Ashutosh Ranka were released from detention by an Executive Magistrate at Delhi Cantt Police Station. They had been held for nearly 24 hours since their arrival in Delhi and were released under the condition of 'no breach of peace for one year'. YouTube personality Dhruv Rathee was also released, with a spokesperson for the Gurugram police confirming that no charges were pressed against him. Delhi Police sources indicated that approximately 2,500 protesters had been detained across the capital on Saturday, October 10, 2026. These detentions were in addition to about 1,000 people picked up in preventive operations across Delhi overnight, ahead of the main protest day.</p><p>The Supreme Court convened a special session on Sunday, October 11, 2026, to hear petitions challenging the indiscriminate detentions carried out by the Delhi Police. These detentions reportedly involved a wide range of individuals, including leaders, former Chief Ministers, students, youths, journalists, and lawyers, raising concerns about the rule of law and democratic dissent. Justice Bagchi, presiding over the bench, described the matter as &quot;exceptional and urgent.&quot; The court also heard a contempt petition that challenged the blanket shutdown of public transport, including rail and Metro services, which petitioners argued was designed to prevent protesters from converging on the national capital.</p><p>During the proceedings, Rajya Sabha MP and senior advocate Menaka Guruswamy, who is a petitioner challenging the detentions, asked, &quot;Is it that an Emergency has been imposed?&quot; Senior advocate Kapil Sibal also sought interim orders. Advocate Sunita informed the court that 150 lawyers, peacefully protesting outside the Supreme Court on October 10, were picked up by police officers in a prison van without provocation, noting that police personnel lacked name tags. Senior advocate Sanjay Hegde highlighted that access to justice inherently includes access to advocates.</p><p>Justice Bagchi addressed several points, stating, &quot;Police officers do not have an independent right over and above the state. Court is agnostic of the identities of the persons who have been injured. Will look at everything.&quot; He also acknowledged submissions on detentions, adding, &quot;We have to hear the other side.&quot; The Office of the United Nations Human Rights Commissioner (OHCHR) expressed &quot;concern&quot; over reports of &quot;mass detentions&quot; at the Delhi protests, a statement that the Indian government subsequently rejected. The Supreme Court bench indicated that the matter would be heard and posted on Tuesday, October 13, 2026, though Justice Bagchi clarified no orders were passed on unlisted matters immediately.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively synthesizes the provided news report, covering the key developments regarding the CJP protests, the judicial response, and the release of individuals.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/cjp-protests-delhi-police-abhijeet-dipke-neha-bora-congress-rahul-gandhi-detentions-cec-gyanesh-kumar-resignation-live-updates/article71570231.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
