       <h1>The Rise and Fall of the LeapPad</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The LeapPad was once the most popular toy in the United States, serving as a smart, interactive book designed to help children learn to read. Despite its success and appeal to both parents and children, the manufacturer struggled to produce a follow-up that could maintain its market dominance.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/the-rise-and-fall-of-the-leappad.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>In a recent episode of the Version History podcast, hosts explore the gadget's history and its inability to survive the later arrival of the iPad. The series continues its examination of iconic school technology, having previously covered subjects like Oregon Trail, the original Chromebook, and Texas Instruments calculators.</p>
