     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-10-11 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>U.S. Military Disables Cargo Vessel in Gulf of Oman After Blockade Violation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#U.S. Military</span> <span>#Iran Blockade</span> <span>#Gulf of Oman</span> <span>#Strait of Hormuz</span></div>
       <p>U.S. Central Command (CENTCOM) forces disabled a commercial cargo vessel in the Gulf of Oman on Saturday, October 10, 2026. This action was taken after the ship’s crew ignored repeated warnings and attempted to run an ongoing naval blockade against Iran, as reported by the U.S. military.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/us-military-disables-cargo-vessel-in-gulf-of-oman-after-bloc.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>A U.S. military fighter jet struck the stern of the Panama-flagged M/V Ocean Molica, also known as the “Arika Sun,” with a precision munition. This strike successfully disabled the ship’s propulsion system without harming the crew, CENTCOM posted on X. CENTCOM affirmed that the cargo vessel had attempted to transit regional waters after departing an Iranian port. Reuters was unable to immediately reach the bulk carrier’s operator for comment.</p><p>In the wake of the incident, CENTCOM issued a warning to mariners in the Gulf of Oman and the approaches to the Strait of Hormuz. They advised vessels to heed blockade warnings, monitor notice-to-mariners broadcasts, and contact U.S. naval forces for guidance. The broader context includes a significant drop in vessel traffic through the vital Strait of Hormuz, with numbers falling to their lowest in over two months. This decline followed a surge in attacks on tankers, which reached their highest level last week since the U.S. and Israel launched a war on Iran in February, raising concerns over shipping disruptions and supporting crude prices.</p><p>Before the war, the Strait of Hormuz was a critical waterway, carrying 20% of the world’s oil consumption. The ongoing tensions have impacted maritime activity in the region. Relatedly, former President Trump stated the U.S. would not resume military strikes on Iran before the November 3 midterm elections, while U.S. Vice-President Vance linked ending the conflict to Iran cutting enrichment.</p><p>What to watch: Continued U.S. naval operations and shipping disruptions in the Gulf.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively synthesizes the provided report, capturing the core incident, the specific military action, and the broader context of the regional tensions.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/us-military-says-it-disabled-cargo-vessel-trying-to-run-iran-blockade/article71570769.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
