     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · NDTV Sports · 2026-10-11 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>West Indies Opt to Bowl in 3rd T20I Against India, Series Tied 1-1</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#West Indies</span> <span>#T20I</span></div>
       <p>West Indies captain Shai Hope won the toss and opted to bowl first against India in the third T20I at Holkar Stadium, Indore, tonight. With the five-match series currently tied at 1-1, both teams are eager to seize a crucial lead. India, led by Shreyas Iyer, secured an eight-wicket victory in the first game, while Hope’s side bounced back with a six-wicket win in the second.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-11/west-indies-opt-to-bowl-in-3rd-t20i-against-india-series-tie.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Individual performances have been key in the series so far. Shreyas Iyer was named Player of the Match in the first game for his unbeaten 102 runs. Shai Hope mirrored this feat in the second game, earning the same award for an identical knock. The second T20I in Ranchi saw a massive 501 runs scored, with West Indies successfully chasing India's 250-run target with more than an over to spare, marking their highest-ever run chase.</p><p>For the third T20I, India made two changes to their playing XI, bringing in Prince Yadav and Nitish Kumar Reddy to replace Kuldeep Yadav and Shivam Dube. West Indies also made one change, with Quentin Sampson stepping in for Akeal Hosein. India’s playing XI includes Abhishek Sharma, Sanju Samson(w), Ishan Kishan, Shreyas Iyer(c), Tilak Varma, Nitish Kumar Reddy, Naman Dhir, Axar Patel, Mayank Yadav, Arshdeep Singh, and Prince Yadav. The West Indies playing XI features Shai Hope(w/c), Kamil Pooran, Shimron Hetmyer, Rovman Powell, Sherfane Rutherford, Quentin Sampson, Keemo Paul, Roston Chase, Shamar Springer, Shamar Joseph, and Gudakesh Motie.</p><p>Indian bowlers will face another stern test, especially with dew expected to play a role. Head coach Gautam Gambhir has previously highlighted the plight of bowlers in this series. In the second T20I, Mayank Yadav notably dropped a catch from Sherfane Rutherford when he was on 38 off 18 balls. Rutherford went on to score 84 off 37 balls, hitting seven sixes and seven fours, and added a crucial 120 runs off 54 balls with captain Shai Hope, which proved decisive in the West Indies' record chase.</p><p>The Holkar Stadium in Indore is traditionally known for its flat decks and short boundary limits, heavily favoring batters. This suggests that bowlers from both sides can expect little immediate respite. The outcome will likely hinge on execution across all departments, with dropped catches and missed opportunities carrying greater significance in these batting-friendly conditions.</p><p>What to watch: The Holkar Stadium pitch is expected to favor batters, promising another high-scoring game.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes the match context, team changes, and individual performances mentioned in the source.</p>
